`translation-privacidade.js` used `innerHTML` to apply translations, exposing a potential XSS vector if translation content is ever sourced externally or from user input.

## Changes

- **`politica-de-privacidade/translation-privacidade.js`**: Replaced `innerHTML` with `textContent` in `changeLanguage()` — all privacy policy translation values are plain text with no HTML markup, so `innerHTML` was never needed here

```js
// Before
element.innerHTML = translations[lang][key];

// After
element.textContent = translations[lang][key];
```

Aligns with the pattern already established in `translation.js`, which uses `textContent` for plain-text keys and only applies `innerHTML` via a sanitizer for a whitelisted set of keys that require HTML markup.

